{
  "image_name": "0a6759110e3a1f6dd1e2e8101bee9561.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts a traditional materials-development workflow with increasingly data-driven strategies for discovering new materials. On the left, the current paradigm is shown as a slow feedback loop: starting from a material concept, moving through synthesis, device construction, and testing, then cycling back based on results.\n\nThe center and right emphasize how chemical space is far too large to search exhaustively, with a notional space of about 10^60 possibilities. In one approach, human intuition narrows this vast space into a small library, which is then evaluated by computation and finally by a much smaller set of experiments. A more advanced approach uses high-throughput virtual screening to define a larger library and then applies discriminative models to select promising regions for calculation and testing. The most automated strategy goes further by using algorithms such as genetic or generative models to guide the search with less reliance on human intuition.\n\nAcross these schemes, the visual message is that only a tiny fraction of candidates can be experimentally tested, so computation and machine-learning-based selection act as filters that focus effort on the most promising material candidates.",
